Etymology
From Ancient Greek σύμπτωμα ‎(súmptōma, “a happening, accident, symptom of disease”), from stem of συμπίπτω ‎(sumpíptō, “Ι befall”), from συν- ‎(sun-, “together”) + πίπτω ‎(píptō, “I fall”).

Pronunciation
IPA(key): /ˈsɪm(p)/
Noun[edit]
symp ‎(plural symps)

(medicine) An digital application to document a perceived change in some function, sensation or appearance of a person that indicates a disease or disorder, such as fever, headache or rash.
(figuratively) A signal; anything that indicates, or is characteristic of, the presence of something else, especially of something undesirable.  [quotations ▼]

Symp was created in 2016 by a university student in Portland, Oregon.